\section*{Equality of microstates and nonmicrostates free entropy}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
A bounded finite self-adjoint tuple is claimed to satisfy finite microstates and nonmicrostates free entropies separated by at least one half, disproving equality even under finite-entropy assumptions. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Mathematical claim 1.} For some $n\ge2$, OpenAI claims operators $X=(X_1,\ldots,X_n)$, each bounded and self-adjoint, belonging to a von Neumann algebra equipped with a normal faithful tracial state satisfying
\[
-\infty<\chi(X)\le\chi^*(X)-\tfrac12<\infty.
\]
The microstates entropy $\chi$ uses the original operator-norm cutoff and limsup in matrix size; $\chi^*$ is nonmicrostates free entropy. Both are finite and separated by a fixed positive gap, so finiteness does not imply their equality.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 298, 2026-09-25): A commuting block signal is perturbed by a small semicircular noise to ensure finite microstates entropy. At a second noise scale, cumulant and Fisher estimates place nonmicrostates entropy near the corresponding Gaussian value, while matrix microstates retain a block event exponentially unlikely for Gaussian ensembles. Entropy data processing turns that discrepancy into a fixed gap with the variable count held finite.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 298, 2026-09-25): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed counterexample to that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 298, the scope page documents the following selections. The selected bounded tuple has both free entropies finite and a gap of at least one half between them, using the specified matrix-cutoff and semicircular-noise definitions. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: FiniteEntropySeparation.lean. Declarations: OAI.FiniteEntropySeparation.main.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

